.\" __fseterr.3 - set the error indicator of a stream
.TH __FSETERR 3 "2026-09-13" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
__fseterr \- set the error indicator of a stream
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio_ext.h>
.PP
.BI "void __fseterr(FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR __fseterr ()
function sets the error indicator for the stream pointed to by
.IR stream ,
exactly as a failed underlying
.BR read (2)
or
.BR write (2)
would.
A later call to
.BR ferror ()
on
.I stream
returns non-zero until the indicator is reset by
.BR clearerr ();
both are described in
.BR feof (3).
.PP
The end-of-file indicator is not affected.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR __fseterr ()
function returns no value.
.PP
It does not set
.IR errno .
.SH NOTES
This function is an extension, provided under the same name by glibc and
Solaris.
It exists so that portable code \(em gnulib in particular \(em can mark a
stream as failed without knowing the layout of
.IR FILE .
Without it, gnulib's
.I fseterr
module falls back to code written for specific C libraries, which stops the
build on any other system with
.RI \(dq "Please port gnulib fseterr.c to your platform!" \(dq.
.PP
Only
.BR __fseterr ()
is implemented from the
.I <stdio_ext.h>
family; functions such as
.BR __fpending ()
and
.BR __freading ()
are not provided.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following program marks a stream as failed after a check that did not
come from the I/O layer itself, then reports it through the ordinary error
indicator.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdio_ext.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("/etc/hostname", "r");

    if (fp == NULL)
        return 1;
    __fseterr(fp);          /* e.g. the contents failed validation */
    if (ferror(fp))
        fputs("stream marked as failed\en", stderr);
    clearerr(fp);
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR feof (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2)
.SH STANDARDS
None;
.BR __fseterr ()
is a nonstandard extension.
